+### Bayer Masking for Loss Supervision
+**Step:** Render all three colors for every training ray, then apply a one-hot mask to select the desired output color corresponding to the measured raw pixel for that ray. Apply a second mask to remove a 4 pixel border from all training images to handle saturated pixels often found on the edges of iPhone raw images [S3].
+**Pitfall:** Using bilinearly demosaicked raw images for supervision causes distracting color noise artifacts, particularly in extremely high noise areas, due to broken 'hot' pixels being dispersed to neighboring pixels [S3].
+
+### Edge Loss Regularization
+**Definition:** A regularization term added to the training loss to enforce edge detail consistency between the current rendering and the target image, alleviating washed-out details caused by noisy 2D-to-3D feature correspondences [S4].
+**Formula:**
+$$ L_{edge} = \| (I_{Current} * G_{\sigma1}) - (I_{Target} * G_{\sigma2}) \|_1 $$
+where $*$ denotes convolution and $G_\sigma$ is a Gaussian kernel with standard deviation $\sigma$ [S4].
+**Parameter:** Standard deviation $\sigma_1$ for the current image Gaussian filter is 1.0. Standard deviation $\sigma_2$ for the target image Gaussian filter is 1.6. Weighting factor $\lambda$ for the edge loss is 0 for the first 15% of training, then increased to 50 [S4].
+
 ## Background
-Originating from the analysis of multiscale rendering and neural radiance field editing, these methods address specific failure modes of standard L2 losses. Area loss scaling was introduced in Mip-NeRF to handle aliasing and scale-space reasoning in multiscale datasets, ensuring that downscaled images do not disproportionately affect the gradient updates [S1]. Masked volume metrics and ray-validity masking were developed to handle the sparsity of volumetric data and the prevalence of background rays in training, preventing the model from optimizing for static background colors [S1, S1, S2, S2]. Density-based losses were proposed in Editing Conditional Radiance Fields to facilitate faster shape editing by directly optimizing the density field rather than composite image reconstruction [S2].
+The concept of masking losses to exclude invalid background rays is standard in differentiable volume rendering to prevent biased gradient updates from empty regions [S1]. Area loss scaling was introduced in Mip-NeRF to handle multiscale datasets where low-resolution images have larger pixel footprints [S1]. Density-based losses for shape editing were proposed in Editing Conditional Radiance Fields to facilitate faster object manipulation [S2]. Bayer masking was introduced in RawNeRF to correctly supervise neural radiance fields using raw sensor data, avoiding artifacts from demosaicking [S3]. Edge loss regularization was added in NeRF Analogies to preserve high-frequency details during appearance transfer [S4].
 
